from ./DerivationStates import DerivationStates, Derived
from ./Conditions import Conditions
from ../model/OpenItems import Contradiction

export abstract anchor DerivationRule as derivation-rule:
    description:: string
    needs:: string
    gives:: string
    examples:: string

export anchor Derivation:
    description:
        Some facts follow from others without being written down. If the
        button cannot be clicked while disabled, and clicking it invokes its
        action, then its action is not invoked by a click while it is
        disabled. Nobody wrote that sentence, but it follows.

        Derivation rules produce such facts. Their results are always marked
        @{Derived}, name the rule that produced them, and list the facts they
        came from, so a derived fact is never mistaken for a stated one.

    limits:
        A derivation rule only takes explicit or derived facts as input, never
        partial ones, as the states in @{DerivationStates} require. It never
        introduces a subject, aspect or value that none of its inputs has. Rules
        are applied over and over until no rule produces anything new, in the
        order listed here, and since a rule can only add facts, this always
        ends. The rules are the ones listed here; a project cannot add
        derivation rules, because each one extends what analysis claims the
        specification means.

    theOneRuleThatSettlesDisagreement:
        One rule, SpecificOverGeneral, settles a disagreement between two
        facts instead of reporting it. A specification often states a general
        rule and then an exception: the button can be clicked, and when
        disabled it cannot. Reading that as a contradiction would be wrong,
        and ignoring either statement would lose information.

        The rule applies only in that narrow shape: a fact with no conditions,
        and a fact about the same subject and aspect whose conditions are all
        state conditions, as described in @{Conditions}, on an aspect with a
        choice fact. Anything else that disagrees is a @{Contradiction}. Every
        application of the rule is listed in the report, so the reading it chose
        is always visible.

    rules: {DERIVATION_RULES}

    example:
        For the Button, derivation adds three facts and restricts two.

        ```text
        d1  when Button.state = enabled: Button can be clicked
            SpecificOverGeneral, then ChoiceComplement, from
            description/s1/f1 and disabled/s3/f1;
            description/s1/f1 is now restricted by d1

        d2  when Button.state = disabled: on Button clicked: Button.action is not invoked
            SuppressedEffect, from description/s2/f1 and disabled/s3/f1

        d3  when Button.state = enabled: on Button clicked: invoke Button.action
            SpecificOverGeneral, then ChoiceComplement, from
            description/s2/f1 and d2;
            description/s2/f1 is now restricted by d3
        ```

        The second disagreement only appears once d2 exists, which is why
        rules are applied until nothing new is produced.

export derivation-rule Inheritance:
    description:
        What is true of a base is true of an anchor that extends it, unless
        the anchor overrides it. Piton's own inheritance rule decides what is
        overridden: a property the anchor declares replaces the base's
        property of the same name, so facts read from the base's property no
        longer apply to the anchor. They are superseded, not contradicted.

    needs: X extends B, and a fact about B not read from a property X overrides
    gives: the same fact about X
    examples: Button extends Control; Control.color = gray -> Button.color = gray

export derivation-rule SpecificOverGeneral:
    description:
        A fact that holds always is restricted to hold only outside the
        conditions of a more specific fact that disagrees with it. The general
        fact is kept, marked restricted, and is no longer used as a
        requirement or as input to other rules.

    needs:
        F has no conditions; G has the same subject and aspect, a different
        value or the opposite polarity, and only state conditions on aspects
        with a choice fact

    gives: F, with the negation of G's conditions as its conditions
    examples:
        Button can be clicked; when Button.state = disabled: Button cannot be
        clicked -> when Button.state is not disabled: Button can be clicked

export derivation-rule ChoiceComplement:
    description:
        Not having one value of a choice means having one of the others. When
        exactly one other value remains, the condition names it.

    needs: a condition subject.aspect is not v, and subject.aspect is one of a set containing v
    gives: the same condition written as the remaining values
    examples:
        when Button.state is not disabled, with Button.state is one of enabled,
        disabled -> when Button.state = enabled

export derivation-rule SuppressedEffect:
    description:
        If something cannot have an action done to it under a condition, then
        whatever that action triggers does not happen under that condition.

    needs:
        on S action: E; and, under conditions C, S cannot be the object of
        action

    gives: under C, on S action, E does not happen
    examples:
        on Button clicked: invoke Button.action; when Button.state = disabled:
        Button cannot be clicked -> when Button.state = disabled: on Button
        clicked, Button.action is not invoked

export DERIVATION_RULES:: extends DerivationRule[]:
    - {Inheritance}
    - {SpecificOverGeneral}
    - {ChoiceComplement}
    - {SuppressedEffect}
